WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:04.458
This example shows a very common design a generated
surrogate key plus

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:10.299
a protected natural identifier PostgreSQL generates
patient

00:00:10.326 --> 00:00:15.078
ID automatically That gives us a stable internal key
that can be

00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:20.895
used in relationships The clinic's medical record
number still has business

00:00:20.921 --> 00:00:25.805
meaning so it receives a UNIQUE constraint The
surrogate key does not

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:31.414
make the natural business identifier irrelevant Run
the example in

00:00:31.441 --> 00:00:35.891
the browser Notice that the INSERT does not provide
a patient ID

00:00:35.904 --> 00:00:41.441
because PostgreSQL generates it Then inspect the
returned row

00:00:41.454 --> 00:00:47.495
and the table structure If you copy this example
into pgAdmin focus

00:00:47.521 --> 00:00:51.153
on the two different roles patient ID is the
internal row identity

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:53.784
while MRN is protected business identity
